@using BlazorRamp.DocSite.Pages.GettingStarted
@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_CSS_Classes}/{GlobalValues.CSS_Path_Part_Table}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Api}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.CSS_Name_Table @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Api_Usage</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.CSS_Name_Table @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Api_Usage</h1>


<p>
    Everything you need to work with the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-table</span> CSS class: the BEM classes it provides, the strongly-typed <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Table</span> C# object
    used to apply them, supporting enums, and the column-alignment classes generated for static tables.
</p>

<h2 class="page-heading">Overview</h2>

<p>
    <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-table</span> is a theme-reactive, static table for presenting tabular content that does not require sorting, selection, or paging. For data-driven,
    interactive tables, see the Data Table component instead.
</p>
<p>
    Colours are theme-reactive: they're built from the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">--br-cls-*</span> variables and shared <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">--br-comp-*</span> component
    tokens, so changing your theme updates <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-table</span> automatically - no separate configuration needed.
</p>
<p>
    <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-table</span> works standalone with no wrapper required - just add the class to a <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">table</span> element. For a fixed-height,
    internally-scrolling table, see the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Table.NoBorder</span> entry below and the scrollable usage example.
</p>
<p>
    Always structure your table with <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">thead</span> and <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">tbody</span>, with header cells (<span class="@CodeSpan.Base">th</span>)
    inside <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">thead</span> and data cells inside <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">tbody</span>. Several features of <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-table</span>
    depend on this structure: the sticky header used by <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Table.NoBorder</span> relies on the header and body rows being distinct groups, and
    <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Table.StripedRows</span> and <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Table.HoverRows</span> only target rows within <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">tbody</span>,
    so a header row placed outside that structure will not be styled by them - but omitting the structure may cause other, less predictable results, such as a header row
    being counted as a body row for column-alignment purposes.
</p>
<p>
    By default, <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-table</span> uses the browser's standard content-aware column sizing (<span class="@CodeSpan.Base">table-layout: auto</span>).
    Columns are sized collectively based on the content of every header and data cell, and text is allowed to wrap onto multiple lines rather than forcing the table
    wider than its container. This means a column with little content can shrink quite narrow, wrapping longer text onto several lines instead of staying at its
    "natural" single-line width.
</p>
<p>
    Header cells (<span class="@CodeSpan.Base">th</span>) wrap at normal word boundaries only - they will not break in the middle of a word. Data cells
    (<span class="@CodeSpan.Base">td</span>) additionally allow breaking mid-word if a single piece of content is too long to fit (<span class="@CodeSpan.Base">overflow-wrap: anywhere</span>),
    since data cells often contain unpredictable content such as long identifiers or URLs. If a header label doesn't fit its column, consider shortening the
    label or setting an explicit <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">width</span> on that column, rather than relying on the browser to wrap it.
</p>
<p>
    To control a specific column's width directly, set a <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">width</span> on its <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">th</span> - the browser will use
    this as a strong hint when computing the table's layout, though it may still be adjusted to accommodate other columns' content. To stop a column's text from
    wrapping at all, add <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">white-space: nowrap</span> to that column's cells; combined with the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Table.NoBorder</span>
    scrollable pattern, this lets wide, non-wrapping content scroll horizontally instead of squeezing into a narrow column.
</p>
<p>
    <b>Known issue:</b> if a non-scrollable table (one that still has its own border and radius, i.e. <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Table.NoBorder</span> has not
    been applied) is the inside a container that uses scrollbars and there is nothing between the top or bottom of the table and the container the corners get clipped.
    This is a rendering issue in the browser, not a fault in your markup. The fix is to add a small amount of padding between table and the
    container - for example add <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Padding.Block(UnitSpace.Four)</span> on the container.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Important: </strong>Given how you need to structure the HTML for a scrollable table scenario (see below) and the need to ensure scrollable regions are keyboard
    only accessible, the caption element is not used. Instead for accessibilty you should add a title/heading and link it to the table via <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">aria-labelledby</span>.
    When you know the table will be scrollable you just add <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">tabindex="0"</span> on the table, and keyboard only users will see a focus indicator as well as be able to activate
    the scrollbar in browsers that still do not support this functionality natively. If you do not want to use visible text you can also use the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">aria-label</span> attribute.
</p>
<p>
    The element that has the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-section</span> class containing the table as a direct child holds the focus indicator when the table with a tabindex recieves keyboard focus
    (<span class="@CodeSpan.Base">:focus-visible</span>). As a side note, the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-section</span> class also uses <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">:focus-visible</span> so if you have a scrollable
    section that has no focusalbe items inside, you can set a tabindex on the element holding the class along with a role and accessible name for WCAG conformance.
</p>
<section>
    <h2 id="c#-classes" class="page-heading">C# API</h2>
    <p>
        <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Table</span> provides the BEM CSS classes for the br-table block.
    </p>
    <section>
        <h3 id="table-class" class="code-table__heading">Table class</h3>
        <div class="@Section.Base @Section.NoPadding @Section.OverflowX(UnitScroll.Auto) @Padding.Block(UnitSpace.Four)">
            <table class="@Table.Base @Table.CodeTable" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="table-class">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Return</th>
                        <th scope="col">Const/Static Method</th>
                        <th scope="col">Description</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>string</td>
                        <td>Table.Base</td>
                        <td>The base class, <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-table</span>. Always required. Includes its own border and border radius, suitable for a table placed directly on the page.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>string</td>
                        <td>Table.StripedRows</td>
                        <td>Applies an alternating background colour to every other body row.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>string</td>
                        <td>Table.NoBorder</td>
                        <td>Removes the table's own border and border radius, and makes the header row sticky. Use when wrapping the table in its own bordered, rounded scroll container.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>string</td>
                        <td>Table.HoverRows</td>
                        <td>Highlights a row's background colour on hover.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>string</td>
                        <td>Table.AlignColumn(UnitColumn unitColumn, UnitTextAlign unitTextAlign)</td>
                        <td>Gets the class that aligns a whole column - its header and every body cell - by targeting the given column position.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<section>
    <h2 class="page-heading">Supporting enums</h2>
    <section>
        <h3 id="unit-column-enum" class="code-table__heading">UnitColumn enum</h3>
        <p>
            Sets a 1-based column position, used to target a whole table column (header and body cells together) for alignment via <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Table.AlignColumn()</span>.
        </p>
        <div class="@Section.Base @Section.NoPadding @Section.OverflowX(UnitScroll.Auto) @Padding.Block(UnitSpace.Four)">
            <table class="@Table.Base @Table.CodeTable" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="unit-column-enum">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Value</th>
                        <th scope="col">Name</th>
                        <th scope="col">Description</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>1</td><td>One</td><td>The 1st column.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>2</td><td>Two</td><td>The 2nd column.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>3</td><td>Three</td><td>The 3rd column.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>4</td><td>Four</td><td>The 4th column.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>5</td><td>Five</td><td>The 5th column.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>6</td><td>Six</td><td>The 6th column.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>7</td><td>Seven</td><td>The 7th column.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>8</td><td>Eight</td><td>The 8th column.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>9</td><td>Nine</td><td>The 9th column.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>10</td><td>Ten</td><td>The 10th column.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>11</td><td>Eleven</td><td>The 11th column.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>12</td><td>Twelve</td><td>The 12th column.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>
            Column-alignment classes are pre-generated for columns 1 through 12, for <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">start</span>, <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">center</span>,
            <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">end</span>, and <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">justify</span>. A table with more than 12 columns needing per-column alignment will need
            a hand-written <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">nth-child</span> selector for the additional columns.
        </p>
        <p>
            <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">justify</span> only has a visible effect on cells whose content wraps onto multiple lines; for typical single-line table data it will
            look identical to <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">start</span>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <p>
        <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Table.AlignColumn()</span> also uses <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">UnitTextAlign</span> - see the
        <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Class" href="@($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_CSS_Classes}/{GlobalValues.CSS_Path_Part_TextAlign}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Api}")">TextAlign API Reference</a> for its values.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h3 id="css-classes" class="code-table__heading">CSS classes</h3>
    <div class="@Section.Base @Section.NoPadding @Section.OverflowX(UnitScroll.Auto) @Padding.Block(UnitSpace.Four)">
        <table class="@Table.Base @Table.CodeTable" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="css-classes">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Class</th>
                    <th scope="col">Type</th>
                    <th scope="col">Description</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>br-table</td>
                    <td>Block</td>
                    <td>The root table element. Sets default border, border radius, header/content styling, and column widths.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>br-table--striped-rows</td>
                    <td>Modifier</td>
                    <td>Applies an alternating background colour to every other body row.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>br-table--no-border</td>
                    <td>Modifier</td>
                    <td>Removes the table's own border and border radius, and makes the header row sticky.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>br-table--hover-rows</td>
                    <td>Modifier</td>
                    <td>Highlights a row's background colour on hover.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>
                        br-table--align-col-1-start through br-table--align-col-12-start<br>
                        br-table--align-col-1-center through br-table--align-col-12-center<br>
                        br-table--align-col-1-end through br-table--align-col-12-end<br>
                        br-table--align-col-1-justify through br-table--align-col-12-justify
                    </td>
                    <td>Modifier(s)</td>
                    <td>Aligns the header and every body cell of the given column position.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>


<section class="@Section.Base @Section.NoPadding @Margin.BlockEnd(UnitSpace.Nine) ">
    <h2 class="page-heading">Usage</h2>

    <p>
        The following example is not restricted by height so had no need of a vertical scrollbar.
    </p>
    <div class="@Section.Base @Section.NoPadding @Section.OverflowX(UnitScroll.Auto) @Margin.BlockEnd(UnitSpace.Nine) @Padding.BlockEnd(UnitSpace.Four)">

        <p>
            As the table could be too wide on smaller screens the parent container of this text
            has <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">overflow-x:auto</span> set on it which may or may not be suitable for you needs.
            The subsequent example will show scrollbars on the table.itself which requires a little more work.
        </p>
        <p>
            The uses striped row and has had each column explicitly aligned.
        </p>

        <h3 id="basic-example">Basic table example</h3>

        <table tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="basic-example" class="@Table.Base @Table.StripedRows
            @Table.AlignColumn(UnitColumn.One, UnitTextAlign.Start)
            @Table.AlignColumn(UnitColumn.Two, UnitTextAlign.Start)
            @Table.AlignColumn(UnitColumn.Three, UnitTextAlign.Start)
            @Table.AlignColumn(UnitColumn.Four, UnitTextAlign.Start)
            @Table.AlignColumn(UnitColumn.Five, UnitTextAlign.Centre)
            @Table.AlignColumn(UnitColumn.Six, UnitTextAlign.End)">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">ID</th>
                    <th scope="col">First Name:</th>
                    <th scope="col">Surname</th>
                    <th scope="col">Country</th>
                    <th scope="col">Date of Birth</th>
                    <th scope="col">Hourly Rate</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>1</td>
                    <td>Gabriel</td>
                    <td>Kennedy</td>
                    <td>Dominican Republic</td>
                    <td>15/12/1913</td>
                    <td>£57.88</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>2</td>
                    <td>Abigail</td>
                    <td>Thompson</td>
                    <td>Guinea-Bissau</td>
                    <td>05/12/2018</td>
                    <td>£26.43</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>3</td>
                    <td>Luke</td>
                    <td>Simpson</td>
                    <td>Georgia</td>
                    <td>17/09/2024</td>
                    <td>£39.14</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>4</td>
                    <td>Sophia</td>
                    <td>Phillips</td>
                    <td>Philippines</td>
